\description{
Grows a random forest (Breiman 2001) of classification or regression
trees on a feature table: to predict each sample's class (disease or
health, body site) or a number (age, a clinical score), to rank the
features by importance, and to estimate how well that works, by
cross-validation and out of bag. The trees are an exact port of
scikit-learn's, and the workflow is q2-sample-classifier's.
}
\details{
\subsection{The trees}{

Each tree is grown on a bootstrap sample of the training samples
(drawn with replacement; the number of draws of each sample is its
weight), splitting each node at the threshold, over a random subset of
\code{max_features} features, that most reduces the weighted impurity: Gini
or entropy for classes, the squared error for numbers. Trees grow until
their leaves are pure or reach \code{min_samples_leaf}, \code{min_samples_split}
or \code{max_depth}. A tree predicts the (weighted) class fractions or mean
of the leaf a sample falls in, and the forest averages its trees: class
probabilities (soft voting, as scikit-learn), whose largest gives the
predicted class (the first class on ties), or the mean of the trees'
predictions.

This is scikit-learn 1.9.1's \code{RandomForestClassifier} and
\code{RandomForestRegressor}, ported to C: given the same random draws, the
trees are identical (the tests check every node against scikit-learn's).
The draws themselves come from ecodive's PCG generator, one stream per
tree, so a forest is the same on any number of threads, and differs
from scikit-learn's as a different seed would.
}

\subsection{Out of bag and cross-validated}{

About a third of the samples are left out of each tree's bootstrap.
Predicting each sample from only the trees that never saw it gives
\strong{out-of-bag} (OOB) predictions and accuracy (or \eqn{R^2}) for free.
\strong{Cross-validation} grows a separate forest on each fold's training
samples and predicts its held-out samples (q2-sample-classifier's
\code{classify-samples-ncv} and \code{regress-samples-ncv}): with \code{n_folds} folds,
stratified by class for classification (as scikit-learn's
\code{StratifiedKFold}), or plain (\code{KFold}) for regression. Both are honest
estimates of how well the forest predicts new samples; cross-validation
also gives ROC curves, a confusion matrix and fold-to-fold variation. A
full-data forest is also grown: it gives the OOB results, the reported
importances and \link[=predict.ecodive_random_forest]{predict()}.
}

\subsection{Choosing settings}{

A vector of \code{max_features} or \code{min_samples_leaf} values makes a grid
(all their combinations). Within each training set (each fold's, and
the full data), a forest is grown for every grid point and the one with
the lowest out-of-bag error is kept (the first on ties). The held-out
samples never choose a setting, so the cross-validated estimates stay
honest. The \code{tuning} element lists every grid point's OOB error.
}

\subsection{Importance}{

\code{'impurity'} importance (mean decrease in impurity, scikit-learn's
\code{feature_importances_}) is each feature's share of the trees' total
impurity decrease, averaged over trees and scaled to sum to 1. It is
biased toward features with many distinct values (Strobl et al. 2007).
\code{'permutation'} importance (Breiman 2001) permutes each feature among
each tree's out-of-bag samples and records how much the tree's
out-of-bag error (misclassification rate, or mean squared error) grows;
it is the mean over all trees (a tree that doesn't split on the feature
adds 0), with its standard deviation over trees. Importances measure
association with the prediction, not effects; correlated features share
it.

Features should not be filtered by their association with \code{y} before
cross-validation: that leaks the held-out samples into the model and
inflates the estimates. Relative abundances are compositional, so a
feature's importance can reflect changes in others; see "Machine
learning" below on transforms (Yerke et al. 2024 found relative
abundances did best with random forests).
}

\subsection{Recipes}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{q2-sample-classifier's settings:} \code{n_trees = 100}, \code{n_folds = 5},
\code{norm = 'none'} (q2 passes the table it's given).
\item \strong{randomForest's scaled permutation importance} (its default
\code{importance(fit)}): \code{importance = 'permutation'}, then
\code{permutation / (permutation_sd / sqrt(n_trees))}.
\item \strong{A microbiota maturity index} (Subramanian et al. 2014): a
regression forest of age on healthy children's samples, then
\link[=predict.ecodive_random_forest]{predict()} for the others; their
relative maturity is the predicted minus a spline of predicted age on
age in the healthy children.
}
}
}
\section{Differences from scikit-learn, q2-sample-classifier, randomForest and ranger}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Random draws:} PCG streams, not numpy's MT19937, so forests differ
from scikit-learn's as with a different \code{random_state}. (A parity mode
that reproduces scikit-learn's draws is used by the tests.)
\item \strong{Defaults:} 500 trees (randomForest's and ranger's; scikit-learn's
and q2's 100), with scikit-learn's \code{max_features} (all features for
regression, where randomForest uses a third) and leaf sizes (1 for
regression, where randomForest and ranger use 5). Tables are relative
abundances by default (\code{norm = 'percent'}).
\item \strong{Soft voting:} classes are predicted from the mean of the trees'
class probabilities, as scikit-learn; randomForest counts votes.
\item \strong{Importances:} impurity importances sum to 1, as scikit-learn's;
randomForest's \code{MeanDecreaseGini} and ranger's \code{'impurity'} are not
scaled. Permutation importance is unscaled (randomForest's
\code{scale = FALSE}, ranger's).
\item \strong{Never out of bag:} a sample that every bootstrap drew gets \code{NA} as
its OOB prediction, with a warning, and is left out of the OOB scores;
scikit-learn predicts 0 for it.
\item \strong{Folds:} as scikit-learn's \code{StratifiedKFold} (classification) and
\code{KFold} (regression), shuffled by PCG instead of numpy. All features
are kept in every fold (q2's DictVectorizer drops features absent from
a fold's training samples).
\item \strong{Float32:} values are rounded to single precision before growing and
predicting, as scikit-learn does; values that differ only beyond that
(a relative 6e-8) are ties.
\item \strong{Not ported:} q2's recursive feature elimination (but
\code{\link[=feature_volatility]{feature_volatility()}} has it)
(\code{optimize_feature_selection}) and randomized parameter search, and
scikit-learn's \code{bootstrap = FALSE}, \code{max_leaf_nodes},
\code{min_impurity_decrease}, \code{min_weight_fraction_leaf}, \code{ccp_alpha},
\code{monotonic_cst} and missing values (\code{NA} in \code{counts} is an error).
}
}

\section{Machine learning}{

Log-ratio transforms (clr, rclr, alr, ilr, PhILR) serve interpretation
and compositional geometry; don't expect them to improve prediction.
With random forests predicting 65 metadata variables in four datasets,
Yerke et al. (2024) found that alr, clr and ilr (PhILR) "performed
generally slightly worse or only as well as compositionally naïve
transformations", and relative abundances did best, by a small margin.
Compare with \code{norm = 'percent'} (see \code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}}) before choosing a
log-ratio predictor table.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # Which body site? 20 samples from 4 sites, 5-fold cross-validation.
    counts <- ex_counts[rep(1:4, each = 5), ] + outer(1:20, 1:6) \%\% 7
    site   <- rep(rownames(ex_counts), each = 5)
    fit    <- random_forest(counts, site, n_trees = 50, cpus = 1)
    fit

    # Predict new samples.
    predict(fit, ex_counts, cpus = 1)
}
